      "traceTextDiagram": "Exec Runtime Native Binary Execution\n└── Runner.Run() entrypoint \u003c-- 2a\n    ├── exec.CommandContext() creates process \u003c-- 2b\n    ├── Get environment from options \u003c-- 2c\n    ├── Inject SKILL_NAME/VERSION \u003c-- 2d\n    ├── cmd.Env = env assignment \u003c-- 2e\n    ├── setResourceLimits() platform-specific \u003c-- 2f\n    │   ├── [Linux] SysProcAttr.Setpgid = true \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/exec/rlimit_linux.go:16\n    │   ├── [Linux] SysProcAttr.Pdeathsig = SIGKILL \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/exec/rlimit_linux.go:19\n    │   └── [Unix] SysProcAttr.Setpgid = true \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/exec/rlimit_unix.go:16\n    └── cmd.Run() execute binary \u003c-- 2g\n        └── Returns stdout, stderr, error \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/exec/exec.go:181",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in agentctl are executable programs (native binaries or WebAssembly modules) that need to run in a **controlled subprocess environment**. The exec runtime solves the problem of executing native binary skills with proper **environment variable propagation**, **resource isolation**, and **platform-specific safety guarantees**. Without this layer, skills would inherit unpredictable environments, lack timeout enforcement, and could leak resources or become orphaned processes.\n\n## Details\n\n### Execution Flow\n\nThe exec runtime starts at `Runner.Run()` [2a], which receives skill input as bytes. It creates a subprocess using `exec.CommandContext()` [2b], which provides **context-based timeout cancellation**—critical for preventing runaway skills.\n\n### Environment Construction\n\nThe runner retrieves a pre-built environment from `r.Options.Env` [2c], which was constructed by the unified runner layer with workspace paths and session tracking variables already propagated. The exec runtime then injects **skill metadata** [2d]—specifically `SKILL_NAME` and `SKILL_VERSION`—so skills can identify themselves in logs and telemetry. The complete environment is assigned via `cmd.Env` [2e].\n\n### Platform-Specific Resource Limits\n\nBefore execution, `setResourceLimits()` [2f] applies **OS-specific process isolation**. On Linux, this sets `Setpgid = true` to isolate the skill in its own process group and `Pdeathsig = SIGKILL` to ensure the child dies if the parent crashes—preventing orphaned processes. On other Unix systems (macOS, BSD), only process group isolation is available since `Pdeathsig` is Linux-specific.\n\n### Execution and Output Capture\n\nFinally, `cmd.Run()` [2g] executes the binary and blocks until completion. The runner uses **pooled buffers** for stdout/stderr capture to reduce allocations during high-frequency skill execution. The captured output is cloned from the pooled buffers before returning to prevent aliasing issues.\n\n### Key Design Decisions\n\n- **Exec inherits `os.Environ()` by default**, so session variables pass through automatically without explicit propagation (unlike WASI which starts with an empty environment)\n- **Resource limits are best-effort**: timeout via context is always enforced, but memory/CPU rlimits require Go 1.23+ or external tools\n- **Buffer pooling** prevents memory bloat by discarding buffers larger than 1MB rather than returning them to the pool"
    },
