      "traceTextDiagram": "WASI Runtime WebAssembly Execution\n├── Runner.Run() entrypoint \u003c-- 3a\n│   ├── prepareRuntime(ctx) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:195\n│   │   └── wazero.NewRuntime(ctx) \u003c-- 3b\n│   │       └── wasi_snapshot_preview1.Instantiate() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:200\n│   ├── compileModule(ctx, runtime, moduleBytes) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:207\n│   │   └── runtime.CompileModule() \u003c-- 3c\n│   ├── buildModuleConfig() \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:218\n│   │   ├── wazero.NewModuleConfig() \u003c-- 3d\n│   │   │   ├── WithStdout/Stderr/Stdin \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:221\n│   │   │   └── WithFSConfig (mount /work) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:226\n│   │   ├── envVars() map construction \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:113\n│   │   └── for k, v := range r.envVars() \u003c-- 3e\n│   │       └── config.WithEnv(k, v) \u003c-- 3f\n│   └── runtime.InstantiateModule() \u003c-- 3g\n│       └── [WASM module executes in sandbox]\n└── Return stdout, stderr, error \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/wasi/wasi.go:110",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in agentctl can be distributed as **WebAssembly modules** (WASI) for maximum portability and security. Unlike native binaries that inherit the parent process environment, WASM modules run in a **completely isolated sandbox** with no access to the host system by default. This creates a challenge: how do you give a WASM skill access to exactly what it needs (stdin, stdout, filesystem mounts, environment variables) while maintaining isolation?\n\nThe WASI runtime solves this by **explicitly configuring** every capability the module receives—nothing is inherited automatically.\n\n## Details\n\n### Runtime Initialization\n\nThe execution starts by creating a **wazero runtime** [3b], which is a pure-Go WebAssembly runtime. The runtime is initialized with WASI snapshot preview1 support, providing the standard POSIX-like syscalls that WASM modules expect (file I/O, environment access, etc.).\n\n### Module Compilation\n\nThe WASM bytecode is **compiled ahead of execution** [3c] using `runtime.CompileModule()`. This compilation step validates the module and prepares it for instantiation, catching any malformed WASM before execution begins.\n\n### Configuration Assembly\n\nThe critical step is **building the module configuration** [3d]. This is where the isolated sandbox is configured:\n\n- **Standard I/O streams** are explicitly connected—stdin receives the skill input, stdout/stderr capture output\n- **Filesystem access** is granted via `WithDirMount(workDir, \"/work\")`, mounting the workspace directory at `/work` inside the sandbox\n- **Environment variables** must be explicitly injected [3e, 3f]—the runtime calls `config.WithEnv(k, v)` for each variable in a loop, since WASI starts with an empty environment\n\nThis explicit configuration is the key difference from exec-based skills: **nothing is inherited, everything must be granted**.\n\n### Execution\n\nFinally, `runtime.InstantiateModule()` [3g] instantiates and executes the compiled module with the configured capabilities. The WASM module runs in its sandbox, accessing only what was explicitly granted, and execution completes when the module's main function returns.\n\nThe isolation model ensures that WASI skills are **portable across platforms** and **safe by default**—they can only interact with the host through explicitly granted capabilities."
    },
