      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Session Environment Propagation Flow\n├── buildSkillEnv() entry \u003c-- 4a\n│   ├── Start with baseEnv \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/runner/runner.go:34\n│   ├── Add AGENTCTL_WORKSPACE if present \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/runner/runner.go:37\n│   └── if propagateSessionVars == true \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/execution/runner/runner.go:42\n│       └── for each sessionEnvVar \u003c-- 4b\n│           ├── env.GetString(key) \u003c-- 4c\n│           └── append to envVars \u003c-- 4d\n│\n├── Session ID Resolution (sessionkit)\n│   └── ResolveSessionID() \u003c-- 4e\n│       ├── Check explicit value first \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:26\n│       ├── Priority env var list \u003c-- 4f\n│       │   ├── AGENTCTL_SESSION_ID (highest) \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:32\n│       │   ├── CLAUDE_SESSION_ID \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:33\n│       │   ├── OPENCODE_SESSION_ID \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:34\n│       │   └── CURSOR_SESSION_ID \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:35\n│       └── Fallback to identity file \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/sessionkit/identity.go:44\n│\n└── Workspace Detection (workspace pkg)\n    └── Detect() \u003c-- 4g\n        ├── AGENTCTL_WORKSPACE env \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/platform/workspace/workspace.go:24\n        ├── CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR env \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/platform/workspace/workspace.go:29\n        └── Walk up for .git markers \u003c-- /Users/jkatigbak/repos/personal/agentctl/internal/platform/workspace/workspace.go:46",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nSkills in agentctl can be invoked from **multiple AI coding tools** (Claude Code, Cursor, OpenCode) and need to maintain **session lineage** across these different environments. When a skill runs, it needs to know which session spawned it, which workspace it's operating in, and which agent is executing it—even when skills call other skills recursively. The environment propagation system solves this by **automatically passing session tracking variables** through the execution chain, enabling skills to write to the correct session context, access the right workspace data, and maintain observability across the entire execution tree.\n\nAdditionally, the system supports **two runtime types**: native binaries (exec) that inherit the full parent environment, and WebAssembly modules (WASI) that run in isolated sandboxes. These require different environment propagation strategies—exec inherits automatically while WASI needs explicit injection.\n\n## Details\n\n### Environment Construction\n\nThe `buildSkillEnv` function [4a] constructs the environment for skill execution. It takes three parameters: a base environment (either `os.Environ()` for exec or `nil` for WASI), a workspace path, and a boolean flag indicating whether to explicitly propagate session variables [4a].\n\nFor **exec runtimes**, the base is `os.Environ()` and `propagateSessionVars` is `false` because session variables pass through automatically via environment inheritance [1e]. For **WASI runtimes**, the base is `nil` and `propagateSessionVars` is `true` because the isolated sandbox requires explicit injection of every variable [1g].\n\n### Session Variable Propagation\n\nWhen `propagateSessionVars` is true, the system iterates through a predefined list of session tracking variables [4b] including `AGENTCTL_SESSION_ID`, `AGENTCTL_AGENT_ID`, `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`, `OPENCODE_SESSION_ID`, `CURSOR_SESSION_ID`, and `TERM_SESSION_ID`. For each variable, it reads the value from the parent process environment [4c] and appends it to the child environment [4d].\n\n### Session ID Resolution\n\nThe `ResolveSessionID` function [4e] determines which session a skill belongs to by checking multiple sources in priority order [4f]. It first checks for an explicit value, then tries `AGENTCTL_SESSION_ID` (the canonical session identifier), followed by tool-specific variables like `CLAUDE_SESSION_ID`, `OPENCODE_SESSION_ID`, and `CURSOR_SESSION_ID`. This **multi-tool support** allows agentctl to work seamlessly regardless of which AI coding assistant invoked it.\n\nIf no environment variable is set, the system falls back to reading from an identity file stored in `~/.agentctl/sessions/active/` that maps workspace hashes to session IDs.\n\n### Workspace Detection\n\nThe `Detect` function [4g] identifies the workspace root using a fallback chain. It first checks `AGENTCTL_WORKSPACE` (highest priority, set by agentctl for sandbox scenarios), then `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR` (set by Claude Code), and finally walks up the directory tree looking for `.git` or `.agentctl` markers. This ensures skills can always locate the workspace root regardless of their execution context."
    },
